      <div class="seo-netsudoku-card">
        <div>
          <h4>Understanding the Core IP Subnet</h4>
          <p>
            An <strong>ip subnet</strong> is a logical subdivision of an IP network. Setting up an efficient <strong>ip subnet</strong> structure is essential for managing security, organizing traffic routing, and optimizing overall performance in enterprise environments. Use our tool to calculate address ranges.
          </p>
        </div>
        <a href="/" class="card-link">Calculate Standard Subnets →</a>
      </div>

      <!-- Card 2 -->
      <div class="seo-netsudoku-card">
        <div>
          <h4>The Usable Host Calculator</h4>
          <p>
            To calculate usable host ranges, use a dedicated <strong>ip address subnet calculator</strong>. Under standard RFC rules, every subnet loses 2 IP addresses: the network identifier and the broadcast address. The formula is 2<sup>(32 - prefix)</sup> - 2.
          </p>
        </div>
        <a href="/visual-subnet-splitter" class="card-link">Try Usable Host calculations →</a>
      </div>

      <!-- Card 3 -->
      <div class="seo-netsudoku-card">
        <div>
          <h4>Demystifying IP Ranges & Subnet Masks</h4>
          <p>
            A subnet mask defines which portion of an IP address belongs to the network and which belongs to the hosts. Utilizing an <strong>ip range calculator subnet mask</strong> tool helps you visualize this split dynamically using live binary grids.
          </p>
        </div>
        <a href="/" class="card-link">Open Subnet Mask Visualizer →</a>
      </div>

