    <h3 id="faq-section-title">Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h3>
    <div class="seo-netsudoku-grid" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <div class="seo-netsudoku-card">
        <div>
          <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem;">What is a subnet mask and how does it work?</h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-secondary);">
            A subnet mask is a 32-bit number used to divide an IP address into two parts: the network address and the host address. It works by performing a bitwise AND operation on the IP address, where binary '1's represent the network portion and binary '0's represent the host portion.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="seo-netsudoku-card">
        <div>
          <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem;">How do I calculate subnets from an IP address?</h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-secondary);">
            To calculate subnets, convert the CIDR slash notation or subnet mask to binary. Identify the network bits (marked as 1) and host bits (marked as 0). The number of subnets you can create is 2<sup>n</sup>, where n is borrowed bits. Usable hosts per subnet is 2<sup>h</sup> - 2.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="seo-netsudoku-card">
        <div>
          <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem;">What is CIDR notation and how is it used?</h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-secondary);">
            CIDR (Classless Inter-Domain Routing) notation is a compact way of representing an IP address and its associated routing prefix. It consists of an IP address, a slash character ('/'), and a decimal number indicating the number of bits allocated to the network prefix.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="seo-netsudoku-card">
        <div>
          <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem;">How many usable hosts are in a /24 subnet?</h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-secondary);">
            A /24 subnet contains 256 total IP addresses. After subtracting the network identifier (the first IP) and the broadcast address (the last IP), you have exactly 254 usable host IP addresses.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

